Imported config can execute arbitrary ffmpeg command
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/9b49f259a284819193c25862c885e028?sev=critical%2Chigh
Criticality: high (attack path: high)
Status: new

# Metadata
Repo: hatolife/ClipForVRChat
Commit: 08dbae4
Author: poppo@hato.life
Created: 2026/7/8 3:18:57
Assignee: Unassigned
Signals: Security, Validated, Attack-path

# Summary
Introduced: untrusted/config-controlled Stream ffmpeg settings are used as an executable path and command arguments without restricting them to a trusted ffmpeg binary or requiring an explicit trust/confirmation boundary for imported configs.
This commit introduces Stream auto-capture by adding AutoCaptureStreamConfig fields and passing them directly to exec.CommandContext. LoadConfig accepts JSON configuration files, and the app exposes both scheduled auto-capture and the new TestAutoCaptureView path that call AutoCaptureRunner.RunOnce. Because Normalize only trims ffmpegPath and inputArgs, an attacker who convinces a user to open/save or test a crafted config can set autoCapture.stream.ffmpegPath to an arbitrary local executable and autoCapture.stream.inputArgs to attacker-chosen arguments. exec.CommandContext avoids shell metacharacter injection, but it still intentionally executes the attacker-selected binary. This turns an untrusted configuration file from data into code execution under the current user's privileges. The issue is introduced by this commit because the external ffmpeg execution path did not previously exist.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] External JSON configs can be opened/loaded into app state via LoadConfig.
- [x] autoCapture.stream.ffmpegPath and inputArgs are JSON-backed, attacker-controlled fields.
- [x] Normalize only trims/defaults these fields and does not require a trusted ffmpeg binary or user trust boundary.
- [x] TestAutoCaptureView/AutoCaptureRunner.RunOnce reaches the stream capture path for enabled stream views.
- [x] A crafted config dynamically caused execution of an arbitrary helper script with attacker-chosen arguments.
## Report
Validated the finding with targeted code review plus a dynamic PoC against the real appcore auto-capture runner. Relevant code path: src/main.go:80-92 accepts an external .json and calls appcore.LoadConfig; src/internal/appcore/config.go:108-112 stores stream.ffmpegPath and stream.inputArgs in JSON-backed config; src/internal/appcore/config.go:274-286 unmarshals JSON over defaults; src/internal/appcore/config.go:420-427 only trims/defaults these fields without restricting the executable; src/internal/appcore/autocapture.go:52-70 starts AutoCaptureRunner.RunOnce; src/internal/appcore/autocapture.go:286 reaches captureStreamFrameWithFFmpeg for stream mode; src/internal/appcore/autocapture.go:423-447 parses config-controlled inputArgs and calls exec.CommandContext(commandCtx, cfg.FFmpegPath, args...). The UI test trigger in src/app.go:495-517 calls AutoCaptureRunner.RunOnce from TestAutoCaptureView. I created a PoC Go test that writes a crafted config with autoCapture.stream.ffmpegPath pointing to /tmp/cfvrc-config-rce-validation/attacker-ffmpeg.sh and inputArgs containing attacker-controlled tokens including a quoted argument. The test loads that config via LoadConfig and runs AutoCaptureRunner.RunOnce. Output proved the helper script executed and received the attacker-supplied args: marker showed EXECUTED, arg=[controlled], arg=[two words], and the diagnostic log showed stream ffmpeg begin: path="/tmp/cfvrc-config-rce-validation/attacker-ffmpeg.sh" args="--attacker controlled --quoted two words --output /tmp/cfvrc-config-rce-validation/out/validation_01.png". Direct crash reproduction is not applicable because this is intended arbitrary process execution rather than memory corruption; the direct PoC completed successfully. Valgrind was unavailable in the container. I built a debug Go test binary with -gcflags=all='-N -l' and attempted LLDB, but LLDB timed out in this environment, so final validation rests on code-path confirmation plus the successful dynamic execution PoC.

